      title: "The Socratic Problem",
      body: `## A man who wrote nothing

**Socrates** (469–399 BCE), son of the stonemason **Sophroniscus** and the midwife **Phaenarete**, was an Athenian citizen who spent his life in conversation in the streets, workshops, and gymnasia of his city, and was executed by it at the age of seventy. He is the most famous philosopher in the Western tradition, the figure from whom all later Greek philosophy, Plato, Aristotle, the Stoics, the Cynics, and the Skeptics, traced its descent. Yet he **wrote nothing**. Everything we know of him comes from others.

If you have taken ANH 101, you have already met Socrates as a historical figure: his service as a hoplite, his refusal to put an illegal motion to the vote after Arginusae, his refusal to arrest Leon of Salamis for the Thirty, and the political background of his trial. In this lesson and the next, we turn to Socrates as a **philosopher**: what he asked, how he asked it, and what he believed.

## Four witnesses

We have four main sources for Socrates, and they do not agree:

**1. Aristophanes.** The comic poet's *Clouds* (423 BCE), produced when Socrates was about forty-six, portrays him as the head of a school, the "Thinkery" (*phrontistērion*), where he hangs in a basket in the air to study the heavens, teaches that the gods are clouds, and instructs a young man in how to make the weaker argument defeat the stronger, so that he can cheat his creditors. This is a caricature, mixing features of the natural philosophers and the Sophists, and Plato's Socrates denies at his trial that he ever studied such things (*Apology* 19c–d). But it shows how many Athenians saw Socrates: as a strange, impious intellectual.

**2. Xenophon.** **Xenophon** (c. 430–354 BCE), an Athenian soldier and writer who knew Socrates as a young man, wrote several works about him after his death, including a defense, the *Memorabilia* (*Recollections of Socrates*), a version of his speech at the trial, a *Symposium*, and a dialogue on household management, the *Oeconomicus*. Xenophon's Socrates is a pious, conventional, practical moralist, who gives sound advice on friendship, self-control, and household management. Many readers have found him rather dull, and have doubted that such a man would have been executed.

**3. Plato.** **Plato** (c. 427–347 BCE), an aristocratic young Athenian who became Socrates's most brilliant follower, wrote some thirty-five **dialogues**, philosophical conversations in which Socrates is usually the main speaker. Plato's Socrates is an incomparably richer figure: ironic, playful, relentless in argument, profoundly serious about the care of the soul. But Plato was a great philosopher in his own right, and in many dialogues, especially the later ones, "Socrates" seems to express Plato's own philosophy, such as the theory of **Forms** and the ideal city of the *Republic*, rather than the views of the historical man.

**4. Aristotle.** **Aristotle** (384–322 BCE), Plato's pupil, came to Athens fifteen years after Socrates's death, and so did not know him. But he had heard about him in Plato's Academy, and he distinguishes the views of Socrates from those of Plato. He says that Socrates was concerned with **ethics**, not with nature as a whole, and that he sought the **universal** in ethical matters, and was the first to focus on **definitions**; "two things may fairly be ascribed to Socrates: inductive arguments and universal definitions" (*Metaphysics* 13.4, 1078b27–29). But, Aristotle says, Socrates did not make the universals exist separately; it was Plato who did that, and called them Forms (1078b30–32).

## The problem

The **Socratic problem** is the question of how to reconstruct the views of the historical Socrates from these different, and sometimes conflicting, sources. It has no certain solution. Most scholars rely mainly on Plato, but on a particular group of Plato's dialogues, which are believed to have been written early in his career and to reflect Socrates's own philosophy more closely. These "**early**" or "**Socratic**" dialogues include the *Apology*, *Crito*, *Euthyphro*, *Laches*, *Charmides*, *Lysis*, *Ion*, and *Hippias Minor*, and, slightly later, the *Protagoras*, *Gorgias*, and *Meno*. In these dialogues, Socrates typically asks his companions to **define** a virtue, examines their answers, shows that they are inadequate, and ends in **perplexity** (*aporia*), without reaching a positive conclusion.

The American scholar **Gregory Vlastos**, in *Socrates: Ironist and Moral Philosopher* (1991), argued influentially that the Socrates of the early dialogues is a distinct philosopher from the Socrates of the middle dialogues, and that the former is substantially the historical Socrates. Others, such as **Charles Kahn**, have argued that the early dialogues too are Plato's literary creations, and that we cannot recover the historical Socrates from them with confidence. In this course, we will read the *Euthyphro*, *Apology*, *Crito*, *Meno*, and the death scene of the *Phaedo*, which together are the most widely read introduction to Socrates. Remember, as you read, that the Socrates we meet is **Plato's** Socrates.

## The man

What did Socrates look like? He was famously **ugly**. In Plato's *Symposium*, Alcibiades compares him to the statues of **Silenus**, the snub-nosed, pot-bellied companion of Dionysus, which were hollow and opened up to reveal images of gods inside: ugly outside, divine within (215a–b). He had a snub nose, bulging eyes, and thick lips, and walked, Aristophanes says, with a swagger, casting his eyes sideways (*Clouds* 362, a line Plato's Alcibiades quotes at *Symposium* 221b). He went **barefoot**, even in winter, and wore the same cloak in all seasons. He was poor, and took no fees for his conversations, unlike the Sophists. He had remarkable physical endurance: Alcibiades describes him standing motionless in thought, on campaign at Potidaea, from one dawn to the next (*Symposium* 220c–d).

He was married to **Xanthippe**, who, in later tradition, became a byword for a shrewish wife, though Plato's portrait of her in the *Phaedo* is sympathetic, and they had three sons. He was surrounded by young men, many from the wealthiest families of Athens, who were fascinated by his conversation, and some of whom, like Alcibiades and Critias, would become notorious.

> **Pause and consider.** Aristophanes, Xenophon, and Plato give three different pictures of Socrates. If you had to reconstruct a person from three such different portraits, how would you decide which to trust?`,
