      title: "The Elenchus: Euthyphro",
      body: `## The method

Socrates's characteristic method of philosophizing is called the **elenchus** (*elenchos*), a Greek word meaning "examination," "testing," or "refutation." It works, in its typical form, like this:

1. Socrates asks a question, usually of the form "**What is X?**", where X is a virtue or an ethical concept: courage, piety, justice, temperance, virtue itself.
2. His companion offers an answer, a definition.
3. Socrates, by asking further questions, gets the companion to agree to other propositions.
4. Socrates then shows that these propositions, taken together, **contradict** the original definition.
5. The companion, refuted, offers a new definition, and the process begins again, until, usually, the conversation ends in **aporia**, perplexity.

The elenchus does not prove that any particular definition is true. It shows only that the companion's beliefs are **inconsistent**, and that he does not know what he thought he knew. But this is not merely negative. By removing false beliefs, it prepares the way for the search for truth; and, by testing which beliefs survive examination, it can give us reason to hold them.

The best way to understand the elenchus is to watch it at work. The ***Euthyphro***, one of the shortest of Plato's dialogues, is a perfect example.

## The setting

The *Euthyphro* is set just before Socrates's trial. Socrates has come to the **King Archon's** porch, where charges of impiety were filed, to answer the indictment brought against him by Meletus. There he meets **Euthyphro**, a religious expert, a seer, who has come on very different business: he is **prosecuting his own father for murder**.

The case is strange. A laborer who worked for Euthyphro's family on their farm on Naxos got drunk and killed one of the household slaves. Euthyphro's father bound the laborer hand and foot, threw him into a ditch, and sent a messenger to Athens to ask the religious authorities what to do. Before the messenger returned, the laborer died of cold and hunger. Euthyphro's family is outraged that he is prosecuting his father, which they think is **impious**. But Euthyphro is confident that he is right. The gods, he says, require that the pollution of murder be purged, whoever the killer is (4a–e).

Socrates, delighted, says that since Euthyphro clearly knows exactly what piety is, he must become his pupil, so that he can defend himself against Meletus's charge. "Tell me, then, what is the pious, and what the impious?" (5d).

## First definition

Euthyphro answers: "**The pious is what I am doing now**: prosecuting the wrongdoer, whether for murder or temple robbery or anything else, whether he is one's father or mother or anyone else" (5d–e). And he points out that **Zeus** himself bound his father, Kronos, for devouring his children, and Kronos castrated his own father, Ouranos.

Socrates objects that this is not a **definition** but an **example**. He did not ask for one or two examples of pious actions, but for "that form itself by which all pious things are pious," so that he can look to it and use it as a **model** (*paradeigma*), to judge whether any action is pious or not (6d–e). This is the crucial demand of the Socratic question: not a list of instances, but the single **essence** common to all instances, which explains why they are what they are.

## Second definition

Euthyphro tries again: "**What is dear to the gods is pious, and what is not dear to them is impious**" (6e–7a).

Socrates approves of the form of this answer, and begins to examine it. Euthyphro has admitted that the gods **quarrel** with one another, as the stories say. But what do they quarrel about? Not about matters that can be settled by counting, measuring, or weighing, but about the just and the unjust, the fine and the shameful, the good and the bad. So the same things are loved by some gods and hated by others. So the same thing will be both pious and impious. Euthyphro's prosecution of his father may please Zeus but displease Kronos (7b–8b).

## Third definition

Euthyphro revises his definition: "**The pious is what all the gods love**, and the impious what all the gods hate" (9e).

Socrates then asks the question that has made this dialogue famous:

> "Is the pious loved by the gods because it is pious, or is it pious because it is loved?" (10a, plain rendering)

Euthyphro does not understand, and Socrates explains with a series of examples. We distinguish between something carried and something carrying, something led and something leading. A thing is "carried" because someone carries it, not the reverse. Similarly, a thing is "loved" because someone loves it.

Now, Euthyphro agrees that the gods love the pious **because it is pious**: they love it because of what it is, not arbitrarily. But then the pious cannot be **defined** as "what the gods love." Being loved by the gods is something that **happens to** the pious, a property it has, because the gods recognize its piety. It is not what makes it pious. Socrates says that Euthyphro has given him an "**affection**" (*pathos*) of the pious, something true of it, but not its **essence** (*ousia*) (11a–b).

Euthyphro, frustrated, complains that their arguments keep moving around and won't stay put. Socrates jokes that they are like the statues of his ancestor **Daedalus**, the legendary craftsman, whose statues were so lifelike that they ran away unless tied down (11b–e).

## Fourth definition and aporia

Socrates helps Euthyphro by suggesting that piety is a **part of justice**. Euthyphro then proposes that piety is the part of justice concerned with "**care of the gods**" (12e). But what kind of care? Not the care that improves its object, as the care of horses improves horses, since we cannot make the gods better. Perhaps, Euthyphro suggests, it is a kind of **service**, as slaves serve their masters (13d). But what work do the gods accomplish with our service? Euthyphro cannot say. He suggests that piety is knowing how to **pray and sacrifice**, a kind of trade, giving gifts to the gods and asking for things in return. But what do the gods gain from our gifts? Only "honor and gratitude," what is "pleasing" to them, says Euthyphro, which brings them back to the definition that was already refuted: the pious is what is dear to the gods (14b–15c).

Socrates proposes to start again from the beginning. But Euthyphro remembers that he has an urgent appointment, and hurries away, leaving Socrates without the knowledge he needed for his defense (15c–16a).

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates asks Euthyphro not for examples of piety but for the single "form" by which all pious things are pious. Why does Socrates think examples are not enough? Is it possible to know what something is without being able to define it?`,
