      title: "The Euthyphro Dilemma",
      body: `## A question for all ages

The question at the heart of the *Euthyphro*, "Is the pious loved by the gods because it is pious, or is it pious because it is loved?", has become one of the most famous problems in the philosophy of religion, under the name of the **Euthyphro dilemma**. In its modern form, it is usually stated about **goodness** or **morality** and a single **God**:

> Is something good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good?

Both answers seem to face serious difficulties.

## The first horn

Suppose that something is good **because God commands it**. This view is called the **divine command theory** of ethics. On this view, there is no standard of goodness independent of God's will; goodness simply **is** what God commands.

The difficulty is that this seems to make morality **arbitrary**. If God had commanded cruelty, cruelty would have been good. If God had commanded us to torture children, it would have been our duty. This seems absurd. Moreover, it seems to empty the statement "God is good" of meaning: if "good" just means "commanded by God," then to say that God is good is only to say that God commands what he commands.

Some philosophers and theologians have accepted this horn. The medieval philosopher **William of Ockham** (c. 1287–1347) held that God's will is absolutely free, and that if God had commanded us to hate him, it would have been right to do so. But most have found it unacceptable.

## The second horn

Suppose, on the other hand, that God commands something **because it is good**. Then there must be a standard of goodness **independent** of God's will, which God recognizes and follows. The difficulty is that this seems to make God **subordinate** to something outside himself. Goodness would exist whether or not God existed, and God would be, at most, a reliable guide to morality, not its source. One could, in principle, know what is good without knowing anything about God. This seems to diminish God's sovereignty.

## Socrates's choice

In the *Euthyphro*, Socrates and Euthyphro choose the **second horn**: the gods love the pious **because it is pious**. Piety, like justice, has a nature of its own, which the gods recognize. This is consistent with what we know of Socrates's thought. For Socrates, the gods are **good** and **wise**; they do not quarrel or do wrong, as the poets say, and they love what is good because it is good. Morality is not a matter of the arbitrary will of anyone, gods or men. It has a rational structure, which can be investigated by reason.

This was a radical position, in a culture in which piety was largely a matter of correct ritual: sacrifices, prayers, festivals, and the observance of traditional customs. Socrates's questioning implied that the traditional understanding of piety, as a kind of **trade** with the gods, was confused. If the gods are perfectly good, they do not need our gifts. Piety must be something else: perhaps, as Socrates hints elsewhere, the service of the gods through the care of one's own **soul** and the souls of others, which is exactly what Socrates believed his mission to be. Some scholars think that the *Euthyphro* points toward this answer, without stating it. If so, the dialogue is also a kind of **defense** of Socrates against the charge of impiety: he is more truly pious than his accusers.

## The theological answer

Christian, Jewish, and Muslim theologians have proposed a way out of the dilemma. Goodness, they argue, is neither **above** God nor **arbitrarily willed** by God; it is grounded in God's own **nature**. God commands what is good because he is himself good, and his commands express his nature. **Thomas Aquinas** (1225–1274) held that the moral law is grounded in God's reason, not merely his will, and that it is accessible to human reason through the **natural law**. On this view, the dilemma is false: there is a third option. Critics reply that this only moves the dilemma back a step: is God's nature good because it is God's, or is it God's because it is good?

The Hebrew prophets and the Psalms already speak of God's goodness as something that can be recognized and praised: "O give thanks unto the Lord; for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever" (Psalm 136:1, King James Version). The theologian's answer is that God is not merely obeyed, but trusted, because his commands flow from a goodness that human beings can, in part, understand.

## The secular form

The dilemma also has a secular form, which applies to any authority. Is something right because the law, the state, or society says so, or does the law say so because it is right? This is the question of **legal positivism** versus **natural law**, and it was raised, as we saw in lesson 3, by the Sophists' debate over *nomos* and *physis*. It was raised again in the twentieth century, after the crimes of Nazi Germany had been committed under the cover of law. The Nuremberg trials rested, in part, on the claim that some acts are wrong whatever the law of a state says.

## Euthyphro and his father

What should we make of Euthyphro himself? He is a figure of comedy: smug, self-righteous, confident that he understands the gods better than anyone, yet unable to say what piety is. His prosecution of his father, which his family regards as monstrous, is based on his certainty that he knows what the gods want. The dialogue does not say whether he was right to prosecute his father. But it suggests that to act with such certainty on the basis of a concept one cannot define is dangerous. Socrates, who is about to be prosecuted for impiety by men equally confident of their understanding of the gods, is in a similar position to Euthyphro's father. The irony would not have been lost on Plato's readers.

> **Pause and consider.** Is something good because God (or the law, or society) commands it, or is it commanded because it is good? Which answer do you find more persuasive, and what follows from it?`,
