      body: `## The question

The ***Meno*** is a transitional dialogue, which begins in the manner of the early Socratic dialogues but moves toward the positive doctrines of Plato's middle period. It is one of the most compact and rewarding of all Plato's works. It begins abruptly, with a question from **Meno**, a wealthy, handsome young aristocrat from **Thessaly**, a pupil of Gorgias, who is visiting Athens:

> "Can you tell me, Socrates, is virtue something that can be taught? Or is it not teachable but acquired by practice? Or is it neither acquired by practice nor learned, but comes to people by nature, or in some other way?" (*Meno* 70a, plain rendering)

Socrates replies, characteristically, that he cannot answer, because he does not even know **what virtue is**. And if he does not know what something is, how can he know what qualities it has, such as whether it can be taught? He has never met anyone who knew, either (71a–c).

## Meno's definitions

Meno, who has heard Gorgias lecture on the subject, is confident that he knows. He first offers a **list**: the virtue of a man is to manage the city's affairs, help his friends, and harm his enemies; the virtue of a woman is to manage the household and obey her husband; and there is a virtue for children, for old men, for slaves (71e–72a). Socrates objects, as he did to Euthyphro, that he asked for one virtue and has been given a "**swarm**" of them. What is the single thing common to all of them, by which they are all virtues?

Meno then suggests that virtue is "**to be able to rule over people**" (73c), which cannot apply to children or slaves; and then that virtue is "**to desire fine things and be able to acquire them**" (77b). Socrates shows that everyone desires good things, so the difference must lie in the ability to acquire them; but acquiring them unjustly would not be virtue, so virtue would be "acquiring good things **with justice**," and justice is a part of virtue, so the definition is circular (78b–79e).

## The torpedo fish

Meno, frustrated, accuses Socrates of bewitching him:

> "Socrates, I had heard, even before I met you, that you yourself are always in perplexity and make others perplexed. … If I may make a joke, you seem to me, both in appearance and in other ways, very like the flat **torpedo fish**, the sea creature, which numbs anyone who comes near and touches it; you seem to have done something like that to me now. For truly my soul and my tongue are numb, and I do not know how to answer you. Yet I have spoken about virtue a thousand times, before many people, and very well, as I thought." (*Meno* 79e–80b, plain rendering)

The torpedo fish is the **electric ray**, which stuns its prey with an electric shock. Socrates accepts the comparison only if the torpedo fish numbs itself as well as others, "for I myself am perplexed, and so I make others perplexed" (80c–d).

## Meno's paradox

Meno then poses a famous challenge, known as **Meno's paradox** or the **paradox of inquiry**:

> "How will you look for something, Socrates, when you do not know at all what it is? Which of the things you do not know will you set up as the object of your search? And even if you happen to come upon it, how will you know that this is the thing you did not know?" (*Meno* 80d, plain rendering)

Socrates restates it as a dilemma: "A man cannot search either for what he knows or for what he does not know. He cannot search for what he knows, since he knows it, and has no need to search; nor for what he does not know, since he does not know what to look for" (80e).

If this paradox is sound, then **inquiry** is impossible. We can never learn anything we do not already know. And Socrates's whole method, which begins from the admission of ignorance and searches for the truth, is pointless.

## Recollection

Socrates's answer is remarkable. He says he has heard from priests and priestesses, and from inspired poets like **Pindar**, that the **soul is immortal**: it has been born many times, and has seen all things, in this world and in the underworld. So it has **learned everything**, and what we call learning is really **recollection** (*anamnēsis*):

> "Since the soul is immortal and has been born many times, and has seen all things both here and in the underworld, there is nothing it has not learned. So it is not surprising that it can recollect the things it knew before, both about virtue and about other things. … For searching and learning are, as a whole, recollection." (*Meno* 81c–d, plain rendering)

If learning is recollection, then Meno's paradox is answered. We do not search for what we do not know at all, nor for what we know fully and consciously. We search for what we **once knew**, and have **forgotten**, but can be **reminded** of. We recognize the truth when we find it, because we have known it before.

## The slave boy

To demonstrate, Socrates calls over one of Meno's household **slaves**, a boy who speaks Greek but has never studied geometry. Drawing figures in the sand, Socrates asks him a geometrical question: given a square with sides of two feet, and so an area of four square feet, how long must the side be of a square with **double** the area, eight square feet? (82b–85b).

The boy first answers confidently that the side must be **double**, four feet. Socrates, by questioning him, gets him to see that a square with sides of four feet would have an area of sixteen, four times as large, not double. The boy then guesses **three** feet; but that would give nine, not eight. Now the boy is **perplexed**: "By Zeus, Socrates, I do not know" (84a). Socrates points out to Meno that the boy is now better off than before: he once thought he knew, and now he knows that he does not know, and so will be eager to search. He has been "numbed," like Meno, but to his benefit.

Then, by further questions, without telling him the answer, Socrates leads the boy to see that the square on the **diagonal** of the original square has exactly double its area. The boy has discovered the answer himself, Socrates says, by drawing it out of himself. Since no one taught him geometry in this life, he must have learned it in a previous existence, and **recollected** it.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates claims that he did not teach the slave boy anything, but only asked questions. Do you agree? Can someone be led to discover a truth for himself, and if so, where was the truth before he discovered it?`,
